{
  "image_name": "3afa13f4c1f5f392322c1f5e13b6b273.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three ways temperate bacteriophages can alter bacterial behavior during lysogeny. Overall, it shows that phage DNA can provide new functions without immediately destroying the host, and that these effects can influence virulence and gene regulation.\n\nAt the top, lysogenic conversion is illustrated as a prophage carrying a toxin gene that is expressed while the phage remains dormant in the bacterium. The bacterium then secretes the phage-encoded toxin through its normal secretion system.\n\nThe middle section shows a related case in which only a lytic subpopulation is induced. Some lysogenic bacteria activate lytic and toxin genes, lyse, and release toxin, while other bacteria remain intact. This released toxin appears to promote host-cell invasion by surviving bacteria, shown entering a mammalian cell.\n\nThe bottom section depicts “active lysogeny,” where phage integration into the bacterial chromosome interrupts a host target gene, inactivating it during lysogeny. Later excision can reverse this effect and restore the host gene, but the outcome depends on whether excision is reversible or leads to loss of the phage. A phage episome with blocked viral production is also shown, emphasizing gene-control rather than phage replication.",
